 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d060: knot (k=9)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.2 and radius 1.88 with 9 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.36) and a 4-dim charge; 13 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 16 couplings (38% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 4 sweeps with slip rate 0.96 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.62 bonds per particle, largest molecule 10.0 particles, 8.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.098 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.099  random bonds 0.105\n  xor      0.512 vs ESN 0.517\u00b10.016  [match, at chance]  no bonds 0.512  random bonds 0.512\n  narma10  0.240 vs ESN 0.844\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.243  random bonds 0.280\n  latch    0.861 vs ESN 0.684\u00b10.004  [beat]  no bonds 0.851  random bonds 0.871\n  mnist14  0.554 vs ESN 0.855\u00b10.018  [below]  no bonds 0.516  random bonds 0.566\n\nGood at: latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
